     "answer": "Total income cannot be used to compare countries because countries differ in the size of their population; a big country would naturally have a higher total income even if its people are poorer. Hence per capita income, the average income, is used instead. However, average income has two major limitations. First, it hides the distribution of income: a country may have a high average because a few people are extremely rich while the majority remain poor, so the average tells nothing about equality. Second, it ignores non income aspects of wellbeing such as health, education, security and a pollution free environment, which also decide the quality of life. Therefore per capita income must always be read along with indicators like infant mortality rate and literacy rate."
