     "question": "Explain the three sectors of the economy and their changing shares in GDP over time.",
     "marks": 5,
     "type": "long",
     "probability": "very-high",
     "answer": "The economy is divided into three sectors based on nature of activities: primary, secondary, and tertiary. The primary sector includes activities that involve direct extraction and production of natural resources such as agriculture, forestry, fishing, and mining. The secondary sector involves processing of raw materials into manufactured goods, including manufacturing, construction, and utilities. The tertiary sector provides services to consumers and businesses, including trade, transport, communication, banking, education, and healthcare. Over time, there has been a significant shift in the contribution of these sectors to GDP. In developed countries, the primary sector's share has declined to less than 10%, while the tertiary sector dominates with over 60% contribution. In India, the primary sector's share has declined from around 50% in 1950-51 to about 15% now, while the tertiary sector's share has increased from around 30% to over 60%. The secondary sector's share has remained relatively stable around 25-30%. This shift reflects economic development and structural transformation of the economy."
