     "question": "What is disguised unemployment? Explain with an example.",
     "marks": 3,
     "type": "short",
     "probability": "high",
     "answer": "Disguised unemployment refers to a situation where more people are engaged in work than actually required. If some people are withdrawn from work, total production remains unaffected. This type of unemployment is common in the agricultural sector in India. For example, a family with five members may own two hectares of land. All five members may be working on this land, but actually only three members are required to maintain the same level of production. If two members leave, the remaining three can continue to produce the same output. Thus, two members are disguisedly unemployed. This is different from open unemployment where people are actively seeking work but cannot find any. Disguised unemployment is also known as underemployment."
