     "answer": "The Right to Information (RTI) Act 2005 is a landmark legislation that empowers citizens to seek information from public authorities. It promotes transparency and accountability in governance. The significance of RTI Act includes: 1) Empowerment of citizens: It enables citizens to get information about government functioning, policies, and decisions. This helps them participate more effectively in democratic processes. 2) Transparency in governance: It requires public authorities to maintain records and make them accessible to citizens. This reduces corruption and misuse of power. 3) Accountability: It makes public officials accountable to the people by allowing citizens to question their decisions and actions. 4) Prevention of corruption: By making government operations transparent, RTI helps prevent corruption and nepotism. 5) Social audit: RTI enables citizens to conduct social audits of government programs and schemes. For example, villagers can use RTI to verify funds spent on development projects in their area. 6) Protection of environment: Citizens can use RTI to get information about environmental clearances and pollution levels. The Act has been instrumental in exposing several scams and irregularities in government functioning, making it a powerful tool for ensuring good governance."
