     "question": "Explain the concept of 'Human Development Index' and describe its components.",
     "marks": 4,
     "type": "short",
     "probability": "very-high",
     "answer": "The Human Development Index (HDI) is a statistical tool used to measure the level of development of countries. It was created by the United Nations Development Programme (UNDP) and provides a broader measure of development than just income. The HDI consists of three dimensions: 1) Health: Measured by life expectancy at birth. A longer life expectancy indicates better health conditions and access to healthcare. 2) Education: Measured by two indicators - mean years of schooling and expected years of schooling. Mean years of schooling is the average number of years of education received by people aged 25 and older, while expected years of schooling is the number of years of schooling that a child of school entrance age can expect to receive if prevailing patterns of age-specific enrollment rates persist. 3) Standard of living: Measured by Gross National Income (GNI) per capita. This indicates the average economic well-being of a country's population. The HDI ranges from 0 to 1, with higher values indicating higher levels of human development. Countries are then classified into four tiers: very high human development, high human development, medium human development, and low human development."
