     "answer": "Disguised unemployment occurs when more people are engaged in a job than actually required for optimal production. If some workers are withdrawn, total production remains unchanged. In the agricultural sector, disguised unemployment is common. For example, consider a family of five members who own a small plot of land. All five members work on this land, but if two members withdraw, the total production from the land remains the same. This is because the land can be cultivated efficiently by fewer people. The extra workers appear to be employed but are not contributing to additional production. This situation is prevalent in Indian agriculture where there is limited land but a large workforce, leading to underemployment and low productivity."
