     "question": "Distinguish between organized and unorganized sectors.",
     "marks": 4,
     "type": "short",
     "probability": "high",
     "answer": "The organized and unorganized sectors are two major components of the Indian economy with distinct characteristics: Organized Sector: 1) Enterprises or establishments that are registered by the government. 2) Follows formal rules, regulations, and legal frameworks. 3) Provides job security, regular working hours, and fixed wages. 4) Workers have access to social security benefits like provident fund, gratuity, and health insurance. 5) Includes government departments, registered companies, and large industries. Unorganized Sector: 1) Enterprises that are not registered or regulated by the government. 2) Operates outside formal rules and regulations. 3) Lacks job security, fixed working hours, and regular wages. 4) Workers generally do not have access to social security benefits. 5) Includes small shops, street vendors, domestic workers, and agricultural laborers. The unorganized sector employs about 93% of India's workforce but contributes only about 50% to the GDP, while the organized sector employs about 7% of the workforce but contributes about 50% to the GDP. This highlights the low productivity and poor working conditions in the unorganized sector."
