     "question": "Explain the objectives of the Consumer Protection Act 1986.",
     "marks": 4,
     "type": "short",
     "probability": "high",
     "answer": "The Consumer Protection Act 1986 was enacted to protect consumers from exploitation and unfair trade practices. It aims to promote and protect the rights of consumers in India. The main objectives of the Act are: 1) Protection of Consumers from Unfair Trade Practices: The Act provides protection against unfair trade practices like false advertising, misleading representations, hoarding, black marketing, and adulteration. It prohibits such practices and provides remedies for consumers affected by them. 2) Protection Against Defective Goods and Deficient Services: The Act ensures that consumers receive goods of acceptable quality and services that meet prescribed standards. It defines defects in goods and deficiencies in services and provides redressal mechanisms for consumers. 3) Establishment of Consumer Councils: The Act establishes consumer councils at the district, state, and national levels to promote and protect consumer rights. These councils educate consumers, gather information about consumer rights and grievances, and help in redressing consumer complaints. 4) Setting Up of Consumer Courts: The Act provides for the establishment of consumer courts at different levels - District Consumer Disputes Redressal Forums, State Consumer Disputes Redressal Commissions, and the National Consumer Disputes Redressal Commission. These courts provide speedy and inexpensive redressal of consumer complaints. 5) Ensuring the Right to Information: The Act ensures consumers' right to be informed about the quality, quantity, purity, standard, and price of goods and services. It requires manufacturers and service providers to provide adequate information to consumers. 6) Ensuring the Right to Choose: The Act protects consumers' right to choose from a variety of goods and services at competitive prices. It prohibits monopolistic and restrictive trade practices that limit consumer choice. 7) Ensuring the Right to be Heard: The Act provides mechanisms for consumers to express their views and concerns about goods, services, and unfair trade practices. Consumer councils and forums facilitate this process. 8) Consumer Education: The Act emphasizes the importance of consumer education and awareness. It encourages the spread of consumer literacy and promotes consumer organizations. Through these objectives, the Consumer Protection Act aims to create a marketplace where consumers are protected from exploitation and can exercise their rights effectively."
