     "probability": "very-high",
     "answer": "The Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Act (MGNREGA), enacted in 2005, is a landmark social legislation guaranteeing 100 days of wage employment in a financial year to every rural household whose adult members volunteer to do unskilled manual work. Its main features include: Legal right to employment for adult members of rural households, Payment of wages according to notified rates (not below minimum wages), Time-bound completion of work within 15 days of demand, Provisions for unemployment allowance if work is not provided, and Works focused on creating durable assets like water harvesting structures, rural roads, and irrigation facilities. MGNREGA has significant social and economic impacts: It provides livelihood security to rural poor, reduces distress migration, empowers women (nearly 50% beneficiaries are women), creates rural infrastructure, and acts as an automatic stabilizer during economic downturns. The Act represents a shift from welfare to rights-based approach in social policy."
